```typescript
export type TaskStatus = "backlog" | "todo" | "in_progress" | "in_review" | "done";

export type ContentBlock =
  | { id: string; type: "paragraph" | "heading" | "quote"; text: string }
  | { id: string; type: "todo"; text: string; checked: boolean }
  | { id: string; type: "code"; text: string; language?: string };

export interface TimeEntry {
  id: string;
  userId: string;
  durationMinutes: number;
  date: string; // ISO 8601, e.g. "2025-01-31"
}

export interface Task {
  id: string;
  title: string;
  status: TaskStatus;
  assigneeId: string | null;
  content: ContentBlock[];
  timeEntries: TimeEntry[];
  createdAt: string; // ISO 8601
  updatedAt: string; // ISO 8601
}
```

**Design notes:**

- **`ContentBlock` is a discriminated union**, so TypeScript narrows on `type`. For example, `checked` is only available on `"todo"` blocks and `language` only on `"code"` blocks. You can add new block types (image, callout, etc.) without touching the existing ones.
- **Dates are ISO strings**, not `Date` objects. This keeps the type safe to serialize across Next.js server/client boundaries, API routes, and JSON storage.
- **`assigneeId` is nullable** to support unassigned tasks. It references a user by ID rather than embedding the user, which avoids stale data. You can resolve it to a full user object where needed.
- **Total time** is deliberately not stored, since it can be derived with `timeEntries.reduce((sum, e) => sum + e.durationMinutes, 0)`, which avoids it going out of sync.

